<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Illyrian Provinces</h1><p class="st-meta">1809 CE – 1816 CE · capital Ljubljana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French-ruled provinces on the Adriatic created by Napoleon, which brought modern administration and an early taste of South Slav unity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Istria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> in 1809. He joined them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Ragusa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a>, already in French hands, to form the Illyrian Provinces, cutting Austria off from the sea.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, first Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont">Auguste de Marmont</a>, ruled under French law, abolishing many feudal dues, building roads and opening schools teaching in Slovene and Croatian. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-vodnik">Valentin Vodnik</a> celebrated the change in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> Revived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a>, heavy taxes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental Blockade</a>, which ruined Adriatic trade, made French rule unpopular.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Austrian troops recovered the provinces in 1813, and they were formally dissolved in 1816, when most became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-illyria">Kingdom of Illyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> a separate kingdom. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> inspired the Croatian Illyrian movement of the 1830s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/illyrian-provinces.jpg" alt="Illyrian Provinces" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1809 CE – 1816 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ljubljana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Napoleon, Auguste de Marmont, Henri Gatien Bertrand</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Slovene, Croatian, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Provinces illyriennes, French Illyria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=illyrian-provinces" aria-label="Illyrian Provinces on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5023 556 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Illyrian Provinces lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5343 812L5437 918M5343 812L5365 872M5343 812L5054 728M5343 812L5381 747M5343 812L5343 812"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5437 918h0M5365 872h0M5054 728h0M5381 747h0M5343 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5343 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5343 812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ljubljana. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=illyrian-provinces">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
